      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50055848",
      "image": null,
      "content": "SQLite has powered countless applications for two decades, but its single-writer bottleneck remains an infamous barrier for concurrent backend systems. Whenever multiple threads or agent workers write simultaneously, transactions pile up behind a shared lock or fail outright with busy errors.\n\nThe sqlite-multiwriter project circumvents this architectural limitation without modifying SQLite internals or introducing incompatible file formats. Implemented entirely as a custom Virtual File System (VFS) extension, it isolates concurrent writes down to page and row granularity so separate writers do not block one another.\n\nUnder sixteen concurrent writing threads, benchmarks show throughput jumping from 8,630 to 49,277 transactions per second, representing a 5.7x improvement. More importantly for production stability, tail latency drops dramatically: the ninety-nine point nine percentile commit time falls from 157 milliseconds down to just 2.08 milliseconds.\n\nEvery committed transaction remains durable and produces a completely standard SQLite database file that works with any normal reader.\n\nYou no longer need to migrate to a client-server database just because your local workload needs concurrent writers."
